{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Franken FDK AAC \u8212- A Manual for the Audio Engineer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A complete guide to encoding audio in AAC / MPEG-4 and to all the options of this encoder. Written for someone who knows audio and is fluent with a computer, but who need not be a programmer or a mathematician.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Document version: July 2026. Encoder: libfdk-aac 2.0.3 + the nu774 frontend, with "Frankenstein" extensions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Author: Micha\u322 ? Dziwisz. Subject-matter consultant: Patryk Faliszewski. Built on open source: libfdk-aac (Fraunhofer IIS) and the nu774/fdkaac frontend.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part I. How AAC Actually Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 1. Why anyone needs a lossy codec\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A PCM recording (what's on a CD or in a WAV file) stores every sample of sound exactly. At 44.1 kHz and 16 bits per channel in stereo, that's about 1411 kilobits per second. That's a lot. A lossy codec, such as AAC or MP3, can render the same material at 128, 96, or even 64 kilobits in such a way that the ear, under everyday conditions, hears no difference \u8212- or hears a very small one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The trick is not about "squeezing" the sound like a ZIP file. It's about THROWING AWAY what you won't hear anyway, and storing only the rest. All the intelligence of a codec lies in guessing well what is irrelevant. That is the psychoacoustic model \u8212- a mathematical description of how human hearing works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2. The three pillars of psychoacoustics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The encoder makes decisions based on a few phenomena worth understanding, because half the options in this program control exactly these.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 SIMULTANEOUS MASKING. A loud sound at some frequency makes you stop hearing quieter sounds right next to it in the spectrum. If a loud 1000 Hz is playing, a quiet 1100 Hz signal is "masked" \u8212- the ear won't catch it. The encoder can therefore store that masked fragment very carelessly (few bits) or not at all. This is the most important bit-saving mechanism in AAC.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 TEMPORAL MASKING. Just before and just after a loud hit (e.g. a snare), the ear is "deafened" and doesn't hear quiet details. The encoder exploits this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 THE THRESHOLD OF HEARING (ATH, Absolute Threshold of Hearing). There is a limit of silence below which the ear simply doesn't register sound \u8212- and this limit differs for different frequencies (we hear 2\u8211-5 kHz best, the edges of the band worse). Anything below that threshold can be thrown away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When you hear that a codec "took away the air" or "flattened the cymbals," it almost always means that the psychoacoustic model deemed something inaudible, and your ear disagreed. A large part of this manual is learning how to shift those decisions toward your ear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 3. Bands, MDCT, and scale factors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AAC doesn't work on samples in time, but on the SPECTRUM. The signal is cut into frames (1024 samples) and transformed into the frequency domain (the MDCT transform). The resulting coefficients are grouped into SCALE FACTOR BANDS (scale factor bands, SFB for short). SFB numbering goes FROM THE BOTTOM: SFB 0 is the lowest frequencies (bass), and the higher the number, the higher up in the spectrum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This matters, because many options in this encoder operate precisely on SFB band numbers. When we say "MS on the 5 highest bands," we mean the bands with the highest numbers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In each band the encoder decides how precisely (how many bits) to store the coefficients. The fewer the bits, the larger the quantization error \u8212- audible as noise or a "hole" in the spectrum. The psychoacoustic model tells it where that error will hide under masking, and where it will be audible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 4. The AAC families: LC, HE-AAC, HE-AAC v2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AAC is not a single format, but a family of profiles. In this encoder you select them with the {\f1 -p} switch:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AAC-LC (Low Complexity, profile 2). The basic, most commonly used variant. It encodes the full band traditionally. Sensible from around 96 kbps stereo up. A safe choice for good-quality music.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HE-AAC (High Efficiency, profile 5, also called AAC+ or aacPlus). To LC it adds SBR \u8212- Spectral Band Replication. The AAC core encodes only the lower part of the band (e.g. up to 8\u8211-13 kHz), and the top is "recreated artificially" by SBR from the lower part and a small description. A huge bit saving while preserving the impression of a wide band. Sensible around 32\u8211-80 kbps stereo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HE-AAC v2 (profile 29). To HE-AAC it adds PS \u8212- Parametric Stereo. Instead of encoding two channels, it encodes one (mono) plus a handful of parameters describing how to recreate stereo from it. Sensible at very low bitrates, 16\u8211-48 kbps stereo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical rule: the lower the bitrate, the more "prostheses" (SBR, PS). At 320 kbps these prostheses only get in the way \u8212- use LC then.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5. What SBR is (the artificial upper band)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It's worth understanding SBR, because many options control it. SBR does NOT encode the upper band sample by sample. Instead it sends ENVELOPES \u8212- the information "in this piece of time and in this frequency range the energy has such and such a shape" \u8212- plus hints about which fragments of the lower band to "copy" upward, and how much noise to add. The decoder reconstructs the top from that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That's why the upper band in HE-AAC sounds different from the original: it's an intelligent reconstruction, not a copy. The SBR options in this encoder let you control the density of the envelopes in time, their frequency resolution, the amount of injected noise, and so-called inverse filtering (more on that later).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6. What Parametric Stereo (PS) is\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PS goes even further than SBR in saving. It sends one channel of sound and parameters describing the differences between left and right:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab IID (Inter-channel Intensity Difference) \u8212- the loudness difference between channels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab ICC (Inter-channel Coherence) \u8212- how similar/coherent the channels are to each other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab IPD/OPD (phase differences) \u8212- these are NOT supported in the FDK encoder (always zero); more on that honestly in the section on PS options.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From that one channel and those parameters the decoder "spreads" the sound back into stereo. At 24 kbps this is the only way for stereo to make any sense at all. At higher bitrates PS usually gets in the way and isn't used.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part II. Bitrate, the Bit Reservoir, and Quality Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 7. CBR, VBR, and what really happens inside\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 CBR (Constant Bitrate) means a fixed bitrate: every second of sound takes up more or less the same amount of space. Convenient for streaming, predictable. You select it with the {\f1 -b} switch (e.g. {\f1 -b 128000} = 128 kbit/s).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 VBR (Variable Bitrate) means a variable bitrate: difficult fragments (lots of detail, transients) get more bits, quiet and simple ones fewer. Theoretically better quality per bit. In FDK, VBR is selected with the {\f1 -m} switch (modes 1\u8211-5), but \u8212- and this must be said outright \u8212- VBR in FDK is weak. The presets are conservative and don't give the freedom that, say, LAME does in MP3.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key thing to understand: EVEN in CBR mode the bitrate locally "breathes." The BIT RESERVOIR (bit reservoir) serves this. It's a buffer: when a frame is easy, the encoder stores it sparingly and puts the saved bits into the reservoir; when a hard frame comes, it borrows from the reservoir and spends more than would follow from the average. The average is preserved, but momentarily the bitrate jumps up and down. That's why "CBR" in AAC is not perfectly flat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This encoder exposes reservoir control to the outside, and that is the basis of our own, better VBR \u8212- see the next chapter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 8. Recipe: a VBR that doesn't cut the last frames "by force"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is exactly the situation you often mean: you have a difficult fragment (e.g. the entrance of a whole brass section or the cymbals), the encoder released a few frames well above the average \u8212- and instead of letting that quality "live," it starts choking the following frames just to get back to the average. The effect: an audible dimming right after the loud moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our solution is a quasi-CVBR: we take the CBR engine (which keeps a sensible average), but we WIDEN the reservoir and the bit range so the encoder can breathe much harder and doesn't have to "repay the debt" immediately. Four switches:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --vbr-reservoir <bits>} \u8212- the reservoir size. Larger = the encoder can hold elevated quality longer after a difficult fragment before returning to the average.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --peak-bitrate <bps>} \u8212- the allowed momentary peak. It lets difficult frames reach high while preserving the average.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --max-bits-frame <n>} and {\f1 --min-bits-frame <n>} \u8212- hard bit limits per frame. This is the core of "constrained VBR": you say "no fewer than X, no more than Y per frame."\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --bitres-mode <0|1|2>} \u8212- reservoir mode: 0 full, 1 reduced, 2 off (rigid bitrate frame by frame).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A PRACTICAL RECIPE for material that should "live" after difficult fragments (~128 kbps stereo, acoustic/orchestral music):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 128000 --peak-bitrate 200000 --vbr-reservoir 12000 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What it does: the average stays around 128, but when a climax comes, frames can reach 200 kbps, and the wide reservoir (12000 bits) means the encoder does NOT immediately choke the following frames \u8212- it lets the quality drop gently. This is exactly that "don't cut by force, let it live" effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Want it stronger: raise {\f1 --peak-bitrate} to 256000 and {\f1 --vbr-reservoir} to 18000. Want to hold the average more tightly (a link limit): reduce the reservoir to 4000.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AN IMPORTANT LIMITATION, honestly: this still works on the CBR engine + reservoir, not on true "by content" allocation like in LAME. The swings are moderate (on the order of \u177 ?20\u8211-50%), because AAC has a hard limit of 6144 bits per channel per frame. But this "light flight" is exactly what you usually want \u8212- and it is fully controlled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 9. Extreme bitrates: very low and very high\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 VERY LOW. By default FDK won't let you go below a certain floor. If you really want 8 kbps HE-AAC stereo or 6 kbps LC (even just for an experiment), use the {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} flag. WATCH OUT for one detail: normally this frontend treats small numbers with {\f1 -b} as kilobits (that is, {\f1 -b 96} = 96 kbps). In {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} mode it takes {\f1 -b} LITERALLY as bits per second \u8212- that is, {\f1 -b 8000} is 8000 bps = 8 kbps. Below roughly 10 kbps there's a natural floor resulting from the AAC headers themselves \u8212- you can't go lower without breaking the format.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 8000 --unlock-bitrate -o out.m4a in48k.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 TWO IMPORTANT CAVEATS for very low bitrates:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 First \u8212- {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} lowers the floor only for AAC-LC (profile 2). HE-AAC and HE-AAC v2 (profiles 5 and 29) have THEIR OWN, hard floor of about 16 kbps, resulting from the fact that SBR cannot configure itself below that \u8212- the encoder will either raise the bitrate to 16 kbps or refuse to start at all. So if under HE-AAC you enter {\f1 -b 5} or {\f1 -b 8000}, you'll get 16 kbps (the program will warn you about this). Want to go truly lower than 16 kbps? Use AAC-LC: {\f1 -p 2 -b 8000 --unlock-bitrate}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second \u8212- WATCH the interpretation of {\f1 -b} in unlock mode. Normally this frontend treats small numbers as kilobits: {\f1 -b 128} = 128 kbps, {\f1 -b 1152} = 1152 kbps. But with {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} the number is taken LITERALLY as bits per second: {\f1 -b 8000} = 8000 bps = 8 kbps. If by mistake you wrote {\f1 -b 1152 --unlock-bitrate} (thinking "1152 kbps"), you'd get 1152 bps \u8212- practically nothing. The program will warn when a value looks like that mistake. Rule: in unlock mode always give the full number of bits (e.g. {\f1 -b 6000}, not {\f1 -b 6}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 VERY HIGH. The upper ceiling is 6144 bits per channel per frame. At 48 kHz that works out to about 576 kbps per channel; in stereo the real limit appears around 600 kbps total. If you want 1000+ kbps, you have to raise the sample rate above 48 kHz (a 96 kHz source) \u8212- then the ceiling rises and you'll reach 1024, even 1152 kbps. This is a limit of the AAC standard itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A note on unambiguity: at such high bitrates (1152) you do NOT use {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} (it serves for going down), so {\f1 -b 1152} there unambiguously means 1152 kbps. The interpretation collision applies only to unlock mode, where you're operating with small bps numbers anyway. In practice these two worlds don't touch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 9a. Is it worth doing an "afterburner plus" \u8212- more iterations at extreme bitrate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since optimization at very high bitrate is your thing, one thing must be said outright, otherwise you'll waste time on a dead end. Inside the encoder there's a loop that iteratively picks the quantization (an internal parameter "maxIterations"; with the afterburner on it gives 5 tries, off 2). It's tempting to think "I'll give it 20 iterations, it'll be more precise." But this loop is a RESCUE mechanism for a bit SHORTAGE \u8212- it only kicks in when the frame doesn't fit in the budget and needs to be pressed down. At extremely HIGH bitrate the budget is enormous, the frame fits right away, so the loop almost never goes into further iterations. Raising the limit does nothing there \u8212- it's not "more polishing," but "a higher emergency-pressing limit," which you don't use anyway. The afterburner itself ({\f1 --afterburner 1}) already selects a more precise tuning table (and this is on by default) \u8212- and higher up this particular mechanism doesn't reach, because there are no additional levels in the codec.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What REALLY gives more detail at high bitrate is not the number of iterations, but LOWERING THE MASKING THRESHOLDS, so the encoder deems more things worth encoding at all: {\f1 --ath-scale} below 256, {\f1 --spread-mask} below 256, and {\f1 --side-bias} above 0 (more bits into the stereo width). These are the levers that actually convert surplus bits into preserved detail (see chapter 18). Quantization iterations don't.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 VERY HIGH (continuation of the proper optimization) \u8212- see chapter 18.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part III. Standard Options (Not Just Frankenstein)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These are the switches available in every version of this frontend. It's worth knowing them, because they're the foundation on which you build the rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 10. Profile selection and basics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 -p <n>} \u8212- profile (Audio Object Type). The most important: {\f1 2} = AAC-LC, {\f1 5} = HE-AAC, {\f1 29} = HE-AAC v2, {\f1 23} = AAC-LD (low delay, for communication), {\f1 39} = AAC-ELD. For music: 2 for high bitrates, 5 for medium, 29 for the lowest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 -b <n>} \u8212- bitrate in bits per second for CBR (e.g. {\f1 -b 192000}). Small numbers the frontend multiplies \u215 ?1000 ({\f1 -b 192} = 192 kbps) \u8212- outside {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} mode.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 -m <n>} \u8212- VBR mode 1\u8211-5 (1 lowest quality, 5 highest). As mentioned, weak in FDK; for serious work use CBR + chapter 8.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 -o <file>} \u8212- output file. The {\f1 .m4a} extension gives an MP4 container; {\f1 -f 2} switches to a raw ADTS stream ({\f1 .aac}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 -w <n>} \u8212- the bandwidth of the core in Hz. Note: under SBR this does not work as you'd expect \u8212- to control the core band under HE-AAC use {\f1 --core-cutoff} (Part IV).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 11. Afterburner and signaling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --afterburner <0|1>} \u8212- the afterburner is an additional, slower quantization optimization algorithm. {\f1 1} = on (better quality, slower), and this is the default and recommended. Turn it off only when you care about speed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 -s <n>} / signaling \u8212- how SBR/PS is signaled in the stream (compatibility with older decoders). The default "auto" is good in most cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 12. Container and metadata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --moov-before-mdat} \u8212- places the MP4 index at the start of the file (useful for progressive streaming). Standard tags (title, artist, etc.) are set with the {\f1 --title}, {\f1 --artist}, {\f1 --album} and related switches.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part IV. Frankenstein Options \u8212- Controlling the Encoder's Insides\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the heart of this encoder: switches that in ordinary FDK are hard-wired fixed, and here exposed to the outside. Each is described practically \u8212- what it does for the ear, when to use it, and what values make sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 13. Stereo in the core: MS and Intensity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MS STEREO (Mid/Side). Instead of encoding the left and right channels separately, it encodes the sum (mid = L+R) and the difference (side = L\u8722 ?R). When the channels are similar (and in music they usually are), the difference is small and cheap to store. It's almost free saving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --msmask <0|1>} \u8212- force: {\f1 0} = separate L/R everywhere (full channel independence), {\f1 1} = MS on all bands. By default the encoder decides for itself per band.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --msbands <n>} \u8212- restrict MS to bands with a number smaller than n. That is, it takes the N LOWEST bands (remember: bands are numbered from the bottom, 0 = bass). Above that \u8212- pure L/R. Example: {\f1 --msbands 6} = MS only on the 6 lowest bands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --msbands-lo <lo>} and {\f1 --msbands-hi <hi>} \u8212- this is a "FROM\u8211-TO" pair. You give BOTH switches and define the range of band numbers in which MS is allowed; outside that range it goes pure L/R. This lets you place MS ANYWHERE, including at the very top of the spectrum \u8212- which {\f1 --msbands} (always from the bottom) can't do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How to remember it: {\f1 --msbands} = "from the bottom up to number N"; {\f1 --msbands-lo/-hi} = "from number LO to number HI." Examples (assuming about 49 bands in LC stereo):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab MS only on the 5 HIGHEST bands (merge the noise up top, leave the bottom in full stereo): the highest bands are numbers 44\u8211-48, so {\f1 --msbands-lo 44 --msbands-hi 48}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab MS only in the middle of the band: {\f1 --msbands-lo 10 --msbands-hi 30}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab MS on the 6 lowest: simpler {\f1 --msbands 6} (the same as {\f1 --msbands-lo 0 --msbands-hi 5}).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How many bands you really have for a given mode and frequency is shown by {\f1 --verbose} (the "active SFBs" field) \u8212- check the top number there before you set {\f1 --msbands-hi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 STEERING THE STEREO BALANCE (the main knob). {\f1 --side-bias <dB>} is the switch to reach for when you want to control how much of your bit budget goes into the stereo width. It shifts the masking threshold of the SIDE channel (L\u8722 ?R) on the bands coded in MS, at the exact point where FDK decides whether a scale-factor band gets coded or dropped to zero ({\f1 energy > threshold} in {\f1 sf_estim.cpp}). The sign is the effect. A POSITIVE value steers MORE bits to the side channel: the threshold drops, fewer side bands are zeroed, and the ones that survive are quantized more finely \u8212- you get cleaner stereo width, reverb tails and ambience \u8212- at the mid channel's expense. A NEGATIVE value deliberately degrades the side: it raises the threshold, side bands fall out, and the image narrows toward mono. Nothing exotic is happening here; this is the very same energy-versus-threshold decision that LAME rides for its bit allocation and that MusePack drives with its {\f1 --ms} control. It is a fixed-budget tradeoff, so at low bitrate you will hear the mid channel give up some bits to feed the side \u8212- that is expected behaviour, not a fault. Sane human range: {\b +3 to +9} for "wider and more spacious"; go negative only when you deliberately want the extreme, artistic, low-bitrate mangling of the width. The value is in decibels (decimal), and {\f1 0} means off \u8212- bit-identical to stock FDK.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 SHAPING THE SIDE CUTOFF. {\f1 --side-knee <dB>} controls HOW SHARPLY a side band flips between "coded" and "zeroed" at that threshold. Stock FDK is a hard cliff: the instant a band's energy drops to or below the threshold, the whole band is thrown away. A POSITIVE value gives you a SOFT knee \u8212- bands sitting up to N dB {\i below} the threshold are still kept (coded at the coarsest scale factor) instead of dropped, so the side fades out gradually rather than switching off abruptly; reverb and "air" decay more smoothly. A NEGATIVE value gives a HARD knee \u8212- bands that only just clear the threshold (within N dB {\i above} it) are forced to zero anyway, cutting the side off earlier for a leaner, more aggressive result. It is orthogonal to {\f1 --side-bias} and combines with it: bias sets {\i where} the threshold sits, knee sets {\i how soft the edge is}. Sane range {\b +3 to +6}. {\f1 0} = off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 TUNING THE QUIET BANDS GLOBALLY. {\f1 --mask-slope <dB>} is a global (both mid AND side) adjustment of FDK's Masking-Slope-Adaptation \u8212- a NON-masking heuristic ({\f1 adj_thr.cpp}) that relaxes the required SNR for scale-factor bands whose energy sits far below the frame's average (stock: more than about 10 dB below). In plain terms, FDK deliberately starves very quiet bands to save bits, and this knob shifts the "how far below average before I stop caring" threshold. A POSITIVE value raises it, so fewer quiet bands get starved \u8212- more detail in quiet passages, reverb tails and decays, at the cost of bits. A NEGATIVE value lowers it, so quiet bands are starved harder \u8212- leaner and hollower, with more bits left for the loud material. It is the same family as {\f1 --side-bias}, but applied to both channels and keyed on energy-versus-average rather than the MS threshold. It is subtle on dense material (it only touches the very quietest bands) and most audible on sparse or reverberant content. Sane range {\b \u177 ?6 to \u177 ?12}. {\f1 0} = off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ms-precision <n>} \u8212- VERY EXPERIMENTAL, and these days you almost certainly want {\f1 --side-bias} instead. It scales the precision of MS bands globally (both mid and side together), on the Q8 scale, LAME {\f1 -q} style: 256 = no change, 384 \u8776 ? 1.5\u215 ?, 512 \u8776 ? 2\u215 ?. In practice its reach is limited \u8212- above roughly 600\u8211-800 the threshold hits FDK's hard floor, and under CBR the bits are merely shuffled between bands, so the sound stops changing. For stereo tuning it has been superseded by {\f1 --side-bias}/{\f1 --side-knee}, which act per-channel exactly where it matters. Kept for completeness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --mid-bias <n>} \u8212- also VERY EXPERIMENTAL and rarely needed. Above 256 it RAISES the threshold of the mid (L+R) channel after the MS butterfly, deliberately freeing bits for the side channel. The cleaner, better-measured way to shift the mid\u8596 ?side balance is {\f1 --side-bias}, which pulls from the same budget from the side end. 256 = off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 INTENSITY STEREO (IS). A more aggressive technique: for high bands, where the ear poorly localizes direction, the encoder sends one energy envelope instead of two channels. It saves a lot, but can narrow the scene.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --is <0|1>} \u8212- globally turn IS on/off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --is-aggression <0..100>} \u8212- a CONSUMER slider for IS aggressiveness. This is what you usually want to use. 0 = FDK's default behavior (very cautious), 100 = maximally aggressive intensity. Why it's needed: FDK has three independent "gates" letting IS in (the bitrate-to-band ratio, the channel-correlation threshold, the minimum number of contiguous bands) and they must be met SIMULTANEOUSLY. Moving one threshold does nothing, because another blocks it. This slider moves all of them at once. Start at 40\u8211-70 and listen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Advanced IS thresholds (for those experimenting \u8212- they act AFTER passing through the aggressiveness slider): {\f1 --is-min-sfbs <n>} (the minimum number of contiguous bands), {\f1 --is-corr-thresh <n>} (the channel-correlation threshold, Q8), {\f1 --is-lr-ratio <n>} (the L/R ratio threshold, Q8). A practical note: these thresholds act COUNTERINTUITIVELY \u8212- a lower correlation-threshold value means MORE aggressive IS, not the other way around.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Placing IS by band. {\f1 --is-lo <sfb>} and {\f1 --is-hi <sfb>} let you RESTRICT intensity stereo to a range of bands: IS is allowed only from {\f1 --is-lo} upward, and only up to {\f1 --is-hi} inclusive. This never forces IS on \u8212- it only limits where FDK may place it. In practice IS lands on the LOW bands at low bitrate, so scan small values to actually see the effect. If you want to go further and FORCE it, {\f1 --is-force-lo <sfb>} and {\f1 --is-force-hi <sfb>} push intensity stereo onto the whole range regardless of the correlation, min-sfbs, and loudness gates. This is a laboratory mode: because IS is lossy and directional (the right channel is zeroed and only a panning coefficient survives), forcing it can deliberately wreck the stereo image. The stream still stays standard-compliant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 14. Band and cutoff (including audiophile full band)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --core-cutoff <Hz>} \u8212- the upper limit of the band encoded by the core, in Hz. It works also under SBR (unlike {\f1 -w}). E.g. {\f1 --core-cutoff 7500} under HE-AAC v2 gives the core 7.5 kHz, and SBR does the rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A note on the effective cutoff in {\f1 --verbose}: the AAC spectrum is quantized into SFB boundaries, so the encoder cannot cut at an arbitrary Hz value \u8212- it snaps the cutoff to the nearest SFB boundary. {\f1 --verbose} therefore reports the REAL cutoff, which may differ from the {\f1 -w}/{\f1 --core-cutoff} value you typed (e.g. {\f1 -w 17300} shows up as {\f1 17915 Hz (SFB-anchored)}). This is not an error; it is the true band that ends up in the stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth} \u8212- audiophile. FDK has a hard-wired band limit for the core: the minimum of 20 kHz and half the sample rate. This means that EVEN with a 96 kHz source and a high bitrate, in reality nothing above 20 kHz was encoded. This flag removes the limit \u8212- {\f1 --core-cutoff} can then reach all the way up to half the sample rate. For 96 kHz material and listeners who want the full band:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 320000 --uncap-bandwidth --core-cutoff 40000 -o out.m4a in96k.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: without this flag there is practically zero energy above 20 kHz; with it the band up to 40 kHz is really encoded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 15. SBR \u8212- controlling the upper band\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Overrides the settings from the internal SBR tuning table.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-start <n>} / {\f1 --sbr-stop <n>} \u8212- the start and stop indices of the SBR band. NOTE: FDK validates the combination \u8212- a bad pair will give "encoder initialization failed." Pick proven pairs (e.g. for 64k stereo start=5 stop=9 works).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-freqscale <n>} \u8212- frequency grouping (0 linear, higher = finer logarithmic). {\f1 --sbr-noise-bands <n>} \u8212- the density of the SBR noise description. {\f1 --sbr-amp-res <0|1>} \u8212- the amplitude resolution of the envelope (0 = 1.5 dB, 1 = 3 dB).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-num-env <1|2|4>} \u8212- the number of envelopes per frame = the SBR time resolution. More envelopes = better-rendered changes in time in the upper band, at the cost of bits. IMPORTANT: this option FORCES a static time grid (it disables the transient detector), so on material with sharp attacks it may be worse. Good for stable, ringing sounds. (The value 8 exceeds the standard grid and is rejected.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-freqres-fixfix <0|1>} \u8212- the frequency resolution of the envelope.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-stereo-mode <0..3>} \u8212- the stereo mode in the SBR layer (separate from core MS!): 0 = mono, 1 = LR (full, independent separation of left and right in the upper band), 2 = coupling (economical: shared envelope + level/balance), 3 = switch (by default the encoder chooses per frame). Force 1 for maximum stereo separation up top (audiophile), 2 for bit saving at low bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-invf <0..3>} \u8212- inverse filtering (inverse filtering). It's a mechanism controlled by a TONALITY ESTIMATOR: SBR analyzes whether the upper band should be more tonal or more noise-like, and accordingly "whitens" the copied material. 0 = off, 1 = weak, 2 = medium, 3 = strong. Stronger = less "metallic" ringing up top, at the cost of some detail. Usually the automation controls it; force manually when you hear metallicness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset <n>} \u8212- the offset of the noise level injected by SBR. Larger values = more fill noise in the reconstruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-header-period <n>} \u8212- how many frames apart the SBR headers are written, which decides how fast the SBR upper band "kicks in" when a decoder tunes into a LIVE HE-AAC stream (Icecast/Shoutcast). Here's the catch: the whole SBR configuration lives in a periodic header, not in every frame. A listener who joins mid-stream hears only the AAC core (muffled, no top) until the next header arrives. Set {\f1 1} and a header is written in every frame, so a decoder locks onto SBR almost instantly (~23 ms) \u8212- the right choice for a stream people join at random moments. Larger values stretch that core-only stretch. FDK's default is about 10 frames (~0.23 s under HE dual-rate, ~0.46 s under LC), and FDK caps the period to at most once per second, so very large values are clamped (e.g. 40 becomes 21 frames at 44.1 kHz). {\f1 --verbose} prints the effective period in milliseconds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 16. Parametric Stereo (HE-AAC v2)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps <0|1>} \u8212- force the sending of the IID parameter (loudness difference): 0 = never (flattens the stereo image to mono-like), 1 = always.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps-iid-quant <0|1>} \u8212- the precision of IID quantization: 0 coarse, 1 fine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps-icc <0|1>} \u8212- force ICC (Inter-channel Coherence \u8212- channel coherence/similarity) on/off. {\f1 --ps-icc-mode <0|1>} \u8212- the ICC rotation mode (ROT_A / ROT_B). Be aware that the rotation mode is {\i signalling only}: both modes describe the same stereo matrix, the decoder just derives it differently. It is a compatibility knob, not a quality one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 PS resolution: bands (frequency) and envelopes (time)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two knobs that genuinely change stereo quality are the ones that decide {\i how many} stereo parameters get sent in the first place. The MPEG-4 PS baseline allows two band resolutions and up to four parameter envelopes per frame, and stock FDK picks both purely from the bitrate \u8212- so at anything above 36 kbps you are locked into 20 bands and 4 envelopes with no way to hear the alternatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps-bands <10|20>} \u8212- the frequency resolution of the stereo parameters. Twenty bands describe the stereo image in finer frequency detail; ten describe it more coarsely but spend fewer bits. At very low rates the coarse grid can actually sound steadier, because each band gets averaged over more spectrum and the parameters jitter less between frames.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps-env <1|2|4>} \u8212- the time resolution. One envelope means the whole frame gets a single stereo snapshot; four means the panorama is sampled four times per frame. This is what decides whether a moving pan or a transient keeps its position or smears across the frame.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps-env-reduce <0|1>} \u8212- and this is the one that matters most, because of a trap. Asking for four envelopes does not mean you get four. FDK runs an automatic reduction loop that keeps halving the envelope count \u8212- four to two to one \u8212- as long as neighbouring envelopes look similar according to a hardcoded error threshold. On ordinary music that threshold is met very often, so the encoder quietly falls back to one snapshot per frame. Setting {\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0} switches that loop off and makes {\f1 --ps-env} mean what it says.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The measurement is unusually clear here. On a four-second probe with a panorama deliberately swept back and forth at 0.25 Hz plus alternating left/right transients, encoded as HE-AAC v2 at 48 kbps, comparing the panorama trajectory of the decoded result against the source: the stock encoder lands at 0.177 RMS panorama error (0.9655 correlation). Asking for four envelopes on its own changes {\i nothing at all} \u8212- the output is byte-for-byte identical to stock, because the reduction loop undoes the request immediately. Add {\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0} and the error drops to 0.117 (0.9944 correlation) at essentially the same file size: a third less panorama error, for free. If you take one setting from this chapter, take {\f1 --ps-env 4 --ps-env-reduce 0}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps-noenv-skip <0|1>} \u8212- a related piece of hidden behaviour. Beyond thinning out envelopes, FDK can also skip stereo parameters {\i entirely} for a stretch of frames: when successive IID/ICC sets look similar it may send up to ten consecutive frames carrying no stereo information at all, leaving the decoder to coast on the last values it received. On material where the image drifts slowly this is audible as the stereo picture briefly flattening and then snapping back. {\f1 --ps-noenv-skip 0} forbids it and forces parameters into every frame.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HONESTLY about IPD/OPD (phase differences): the FDK encoder does not emit them. The phase fields are hardcoded to zero, with the comment "IPD OPD not supported right now". It is worth knowing exactly how far from finished that is, though, because it is closer than the comment suggests. The encoder already accumulates both the real and the imaginary part of the left/right cross-spectrum for every band and envelope, and then throws the phase away \u8212- only the magnitude is used, to compute ICC. The Huffman tables and bitstream writers for IPD and OPD are also already present and complete. So the missing piece is not "phase analysis from scratch"; it is deriving an angle from two numbers that are already there, quantising it, and enabling the existing writer. Whether that is worth doing is a separate question from whether it is hard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 SBR detectors: attacks and fabricated harmonics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two detectors inside SBR decide most of what the replicated high band actually sounds like, and until now neither was reachable from the command line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\b transient detector} decides whether an attack gets its own envelope or is averaged into a longer stretch. When it misses an attack, the energy of the hit gets spread backwards in time \u8212- you hear a short burst of high-frequency hiss just {\i before} the hi-hat, not after it. That is pre-echo, and in the SBR band it is one of the more recognisable coding artefacts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr <n>} scales the master threshold that decides how easily the detector fires (value*100, so 100 is unchanged). This turned out to be the single most effective knob in the group. Measured on a signal of 24 percussive attacks plus decaying tones between 8 and 13 kHz, counting high-band energy that appears before an attack but is absent from the source: stock scores 1.610 at 32 kbps, and lowering the threshold to 40 drops that to 0.041 \u8212- pre-echo essentially gone. The same thing happens at 64 kbps (1.186 to 0.059). The effect saturates below 60 and disappears entirely at 100 and above, where output is byte-identical to stock. On percussive material {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr 40} is the place to start.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak <n>} controls how {\i peaky} a candidate must be to count as an attack \u8212- the raw 0.90 constant, entered as 90. {\f1 --sbr-tran-split <n>} governs how readily a frame with no detected transient is still split into two envelopes, which buys time resolution at the cost of bits. Two further knobs, {\f1 --sbr-tran-quiet} and {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom}, exist for completeness but do nothing in this build: they live in the fast transient detector, which is only used for AAC-LD and ELD, and those profiles fail to initialise here \u8212- in the stock binary too, so it is an inherited limitation rather than something these knobs broke.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\b missing harmonics detector} handles the opposite problem. When SBR copies a low band upwards, a tone that existed in the original can land in the wrong place or vanish. The detector notices this and fabricates a synthetic harmonic to put it back. Set it too eagerly and you get whistling and metallic artefacts that were never in the recording; set it too conservatively and bells, glockenspiel and cymbals go dull as their partials quietly disappear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone <n>} scales how tonal a band must be before a harmonic is added, and is the main control here. {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff <n>} scales the tonality {\i difference} between the original and the patched band that triggers compensation. {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig} and {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-diff} change how long an already-found tone keeps being tracked as it fades, which is what you hear on cymbal and bell tails. {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr} and {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig} move the spectral-flatness line between "this is a tone" and "this is noise", for the patched and the original band respectively. {\f1 --sbr-mh-maxcomp} caps how much envelope compensation is applied around an added harmonic, which affects how much noise sits next to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, {\f1 --sbr-noise-max <6|3|-3>} sets the ceiling on how loud the noise SBR injects may get \u8212- 1.0, 0.5 or 0.125 respectively. This is the straightest control over "air versus hiss" in the top end. It was already a configuration field inside FDK, chosen from the bitrate tuning table and never exposed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of these are opt-in: at their neutral values the output is bit-identical to stock, and the test suite enforces that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 17. TNS, PNS, and the afterburner\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --tns-mask <n>} / {\f1 --tns-order <n>} \u8212- TNS (Temporal Noise Shaping) shapes the quantization noise in time, so it hides under transients (important for sharp attacks, e.g. percussion). The mask is a bitmap of active filters, the order is the length of the filter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --pns <0|1>} \u8212- PNS (Perceptual Noise Substitution) replaces noise bands with the description "there's noise of such energy here" instead of encoding it precisely. A big saving on noise material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --pns-start <Hz>} \u8212- from what frequency PNS may act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --force-pns} \u8212- bypass the low-bitrate gate for PNS. EXPLANATION: FDK has a table that at very low bitrates (below about 28 kbps) completely disables PNS \u8212- and then {\f1 --pns-start} is ignored, because PNS doesn't work at all. That's why at 24 kbps you heard artifacts like from MP3, and at 64 kbps PNS worked. This flag bypasses the table and turns on PNS despite the low bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A word on the units before the individual knobs, because it makes the rest make sense. FDK keeps these PNS detection parameters as fixed-point integers internally, and our switches take a plain decimal MULTIPLIER: whatever you type is multiplied by 100 and used to scale FDK's factory value. So {\f1 1.0} means "\u215 ?1.0 = leave the factory value alone", {\f1 1.5} means "\u215 ?1.5", {\f1 0.5} means "\u215 ?0.5", and {\f1 -1} means the switch is off entirely. That single convention ({\f1 value \u215 ? 100}, {\f1 1.0} = no change) applies to every {\f1 --pns-*} scaling knob below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --pns-gain <x>} \u8212- the LOUDNESS of the fabricated PNS noise, and the one PNS knob you'll reach for most. When PNS decides a band is "just noise", it throws away the actual spectral lines and stores only a single number: the noise energy of that band. On playback the decoder regenerates random noise scaled to that energy. {\f1 --pns-gain} scales that stored energy directly. {\f1 1.0} = unchanged (the regenerated noise carries the original band's energy); {\f1 >1.0} makes the noise fill louder than the original (useful when the substituted noise sounds too timid and the mix goes dull); {\f1 <1.0} makes it quieter (when the noise fill hisses too much). Think of it as the "how loud is the substituted noise" dial. Decimal input, {\f1 -1} = off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The remaining PNS knobs govern WHICH bands get turned into noise in the first place \u8212- the detection stage \u8212- rather than how loud the result is. {\f1 --pns-tonality <x>} scales the tonality detection threshold. PNS only fires on bands the encoder judges "noise-like" (low tonality); raising this threshold lets more bands \u8212- even somewhat tonal ones \u8212- qualify as noise, so the noise substitution gets WIDER (more of the spectrum replaced by cheap noise, more saving but also more risk of smearing genuine tones). Lowering it makes PNS pickier. {\f1 1.0} = default.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --pns-refpower <x>} scales the reference-power detection threshold. PNS compares each band's power against a reference before deciding it is a noise candidate; this knob moves that power gate. {\f1 1.0} = default; it interacts with tonality \u8212- both must agree for a band to become PNS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --pns-gapfill <x>} scales the gap-fill threshold. When PNS has marked bands on either side of a small gap, this heuristic can "close the gap" and mark the band between them as PNS too, so you don't get a coded island stranded between two noise bands. Advanced and subtle \u8212- rarely audible on its own. {\f1 1.0} = default.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --pns-min-width <n>} sets the minimum SFB width, in spectral lines, that a band must have before PNS is allowed to act on it. This one is a raw line count, not a {\f1 \u215 ?100} multiplier. It is effective only above the built-in default (LC = 16 lines); pushing it to 32 or 64 restricts PNS to the wider bands only, keeping the encoder from substituting noise on narrow low bands where it would be more obvious. {\f1 -1} = off (use FDK's default).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ath-scale <n>} \u8212- scaling of the threshold of hearing (ATH), in Q8 (256 = \u215 ?1.0). This is the GLOBAL masking regulator. Above 256 = raises the thresholds = the encoder deems more things inaudible = more aggressive, fewer bits. Below 256 = lowers the thresholds = the encoder preserves more detail = more bits. This is the simplest way to tell the encoder "be cleaner" or "be more economical."\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part V. Details at High Bitrate and Tuning for Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 18. "More bits = more detail": how to really squeeze it out\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question that often comes up: since I'm giving 400 kbps, will the encoder really use it on a better description of details, or does part get wasted? In AAC there aren't such advanced, separate regulators as in MusePack (signal-to-mask ratio, tone-masks-noise, noise-masks-tone as separate knobs). But the effect "describe more precisely, mask less" we achieve with two tools that together do exactly the same thing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ath-scale <n>} BELOW 256 \u8212- lowers the global threshold of hearing. The encoder stops assuming something is inaudible and starts encoding it precisely. This is the closest equivalent of lowering the masking threshold from MusePack. At 320\u8211-400 kbps try 200 or 180.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --spread-mask <n>} BELOW 256 \u8212- controls the SPREADING of masking between neighboring bands. In psychoacoustics a loud band "spreads" its masking ability onto its neighbors (that's exactly tone-masks-noise). By reducing this parameter, you tell the encoder: assume less that neighboring bands mask each other \u8212- so more bands are treated as audible and precisely encoded. The biggest effect where bits are the limit (96\u8211-192 kbps); at very high bitrate the encoder has a surplus of bits anyway, so the change tends to be small.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An audiophile RECIPE (maximum detail, high bitrate, material with a rich top):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 400000 --ath-scale 190 --spread-mask 128 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The combination: a lowered global threshold (ath-scale 190) + reduced spreading of masking (spread-mask 128). The encoder preserves considerably more microdetail, which in a phase comparison with the original gives smaller differences. This is in the spirit of what MusePack did at 1000 kbps \u8212- within the bounds of what AAC allows exposing without rewriting the whole model.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For material where you want more of the budget in the stereo width, add {\f1 --side-bias 6} \u8212- steering bits into the side channel is another place where a high bitrate really gives better sound. (The old {\f1 --ms-precision} knob did something similar globally but is very experimental and largely superseded; {\f1 --side-bias} is the right tool now.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond ath-scale there's a more direct lever, and it's worth understanding the units. These min-SNR knobs work on the Q8 fixed-point scale, where 256 stands for 1.0 (so 256 = "leave it alone", 128 = \u215 ?0.5, 512 = \u215 ?2.0); {\f1 -1} means the switch is off. {\f1 --minsnr-scale <n>} (Q8, 256 = off) is the closest thing FDK has to MusePack's TMN/NMT knobs: it scales the REQUIRED per-band coding SNR ({\f1 sfbMinSnrLdData}), i.e. the minimum signal-to-noise ratio the encoder insists on achieving in each scale-factor band. Values BELOW 256 demand a HIGHER SNR \u8212- the encoder has to code each band more accurately, so more detail and more bits \u8212- while values above 256 relax the demand and code more coarsely. It's more effective than {\f1 --ath-scale} because min-SNR is exactly the floor that FDK's "avoid holes" logic clamps the thresholds back to; touching the threshold copy alone (as ath-scale does) is partly undone downstream, but the min-SNR floor is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two clamp knobs move the hard limits that FDK puts around that required SNR. Internally FDK never lets a band demand more than a MAX_SNR ceiling (about \u8722 ?1 dB) nor less than a MIN_SNR floor (about \u8722 ?25 dB), so even an aggressive {\f1 --minsnr-scale} is capped by these. {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi <n>} (Q8, 256 = off) scales the MAX_SNR ceiling, letting demanding bands ask for MORE than the factory cap \u8212- this is what you raise if {\f1 --minsnr-scale} alone seems to "run out of room" at the top. {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo <n>} (Q8, 256 = off) scales the MIN_SNR floor at the other end. Together they widen FDK's factory window so {\f1 --minsnr-scale} has somewhere further to push.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, {\f1 --reduce-clamp 0} removes the "29 dB Ratio" ceiling on threshold reduction inside the CBR quantizer. When the CBR bit-allocation loop is short on bits it raises (loosens) thresholds, but stock FDK refuses to reduce a threshold by more than about 29 dB relative to the reference \u8212- a safety clamp. Setting {\f1 --reduce-clamp 0} lifts that clamp, letting the encoder push thresholds deeper and pour bits into the most demanding bands. It pairs naturally with {\f1 --minsnr-scale} for extreme detail, and it only affects CBR (VBR runs a different path). The default is {\f1 1} (clamp on, stock behaviour).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 19. Tuning for human speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yes, in HE-AAC (specifically in the SBR layer) there's a separate tuning mode for speech. In ordinary FDK it's hard-wired as off \u8212- here we've exposed it with a flag:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --speech} \u8212- turns on SBR tuning for human speech. It changes the inverse-filtering thresholds, the noise level, and disables parametric encoding of the upper band \u8212- all so that speech (podcast, audiobook, dialogue) at low bitrate sounds more natural, without "gurgling" up top. It applies to HE-AAC/HE-AAC v2 (because it works in SBR).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 5 -b 32000 --speech -o mowa.m4a podcast.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Honestly about LC: pure AAC-LC (without SBR) does NOT have a separate, distinct "speech mode" in FDK. The LC psychoacoustic model is the same for speech and music. For speech in LC it's best to simply lower the band ({\f1 --core-cutoff} e.g. 12000\u8211-14000 Hz, because speech doesn't have much above that) and possibly turn on {\f1 --pns} with {\f1 --force-pns} at very low bitrates. But there's no dedicated "speech" switch for LC, because it's not in the codec itself \u8212- and I don't want to pretend it is.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part VI. Ready-Made Recipes (From Extreme to Extreme)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 20. A set of practical recipes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MUSIC, HIGH ARCHIVAL QUALITY (transparent stereo):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 256000 --afterburner 1 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MUSIC, "BREATHING" QUASI-VBR (doesn't cut after climaxes):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 160000 --peak-bitrate 256000 --vbr-reservoir 16000 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AUDIOPHILE, FULL BAND 96 kHz + maximum detail:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 400000 --uncap-bandwidth --core-cutoff 40000 --ath-scale 190 --spread-mask 128 -o out.m4a in96k.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MUSIC STREAMING, MEDIUM BITRATE:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-stereo-mode 3 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PODCAST / SPEECH, LOW BITRATE:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 5 -b 32000 --speech --core-cutoff 12000 -o out.m4a mowa.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 EXTREMELY LOW (experiment, 8 kbps stereo):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 8000 --unlock-bitrate -o out.m4a in48k.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 RESCUING STEREO AT LOW BITRATE (aggressive intensity):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 96000 --is 1 --is-aggression 70 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MS ONLY UP TOP (bottom full stereo, top merged):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 128000 --msbands-lo 44 --msbands-hi 48 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 21. How to approach your own experiments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Change ONE parameter at a time and listen (or compare the spectrum/phase with the original). The encoder with no Frankenstein flags behaves exactly like the original FDK \u8212- that's your reference point. Every flag is a deliberate deviation from the default, proven behavior.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The order worth trying when there are problems:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab "too little detail / too flat" \u8594 ? {\f1 --ath-scale} down, then {\f1 --spread-mask} down.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab "stereo too narrow up top" \u8594 ? {\f1 --sbr-stereo-mode 1} (HE-AAC) or less aggressive IS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab "metallic highs" \u8594 ? {\f1 --sbr-invf 2} or 3.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab "cuts after loud fragments" \u8594 ? {\f1 --peak-bitrate} + {\f1 --vbr-reservoir} (chapter 8).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab "artifacts like MP3 at very low bitrate" \u8594 ? {\f1 --force-pns}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab "I want the full band from 96 kHz" \u8594 ? {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth --core-cutoff 40000}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 22. A closing note on integrity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All the behaviors described here were verified by measurement (stream decodability, real bitrate spread, spectral content), not just assumed. Where something has a limitation (IPD/OPD unsupported, no speech mode in LC, the ceiling of 6144 bits per channel, a residual bitrate floor of ~10 kbps), it is said outright, instead of promising things the codec can't do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The encoder without flags = pure FDK. The flags = your deliberate decisions. Happy tuning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part VII. Reference Tables\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three orientation tables computed from the tuning tables in the FDK code. They are APPROXIMATE (the band grid is stepped), but they show the right order of magnitude and help you deliberately choose settings without guessing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 23. Table 1 \u8212- the upper frequency of the band (SFB)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The spectrum is divided into bands (scale factor bands, SFB), numbered from the bottom: band 0 is the lowest bass, the higher the number, the higher in frequency. When you restrict something to "N bands" ({\f1 --msbands}, {\f1 --isbands}), this table tells you what frequency that roughly corresponds to. Every fourth band is shown; the last row is the total number of bands and the Nyquist frequency (half the sample rate).\par}
