{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An example of how to read it: at 44.1 kHz band no. 40 ends around 15.2 kHz. If you want MS to work only below ~15 kHz, set {\f1 --msbands 40}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 24. Table 2 \u8212- SBR: the start index vs. the crossover frequency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In HE-AAC the lower part of the band is encoded by the AAC core, and the upper part is added by SBR. {\f1 --sbr-start} (index 0..15) decides at what frequency SBR begins \u8212- a lower index means SBR comes in lower, so the core is narrower. "core" is the core's sample rate; in dual-rate mode the output is twice as high as the core.\par}
{
