{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical conclusion: in pure AAC-LC (without SBR) the automation stops around 17 kHz anyway. Giving {\f1 -w} higher makes sense only with a large bitrate reserve; a full band above 20 kHz requires {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth} and a sample rate of at least 96 kHz.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 26. How to read {\f1 --verbose}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --verbose} prints raw values without hints in parentheses, so the readout is clean. Here's what the less obvious ones mean:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab AOT (profile): 2 = AAC-LC, 5 = HE-AAC, 29 = HE-AAC v2, 23 = AAC-LD, 39 = AAC-ELD.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab bitrate-mode: 0 = CBR, 1 to 5 = VBR (higher = better quality).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab channel-mode: 1 = mono, 2 = stereo. In HE-AAC v2 the core is mono, and stereo is recreated from parameters (Parametric Stereo).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab core bandwidth: the upper frequency of the AAC core. The SOURCE is given in parentheses: "from -w" (you gave it manually), "from --core-cutoff" or "auto". Under SBR this is only the core \u8212- SBR plays above this value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab final BW (AAC+SBR): shown only with SBR active \u8212- the approximate upper frequency of the WHOLE signal (core plus SBR), computed from the SBR stop index. It's a complement to "core bandwidth": that says how far the core alone reaches, and this how far the whole HE-AAC reaches after adding SBR.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab sbr-ratio: 1 = single-rate (downsampled), 2 = dual-rate (the core at half the output frequency).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab sbr amp res: 0 = resolution 1.5 dB, 1 = 3.0 dB.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab codec delay: the codec delay in samples per channel \u8212- important for gapless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab IS corr threshold, IS L/R ratio: thresholds on the Q8 scale, where 256 = 1.0. A lower correlation threshold means intensity stereo turns on MORE READILY (contrary to intuition).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab franken overrides applied: a list of the switches that in this run deviate from pure FDK (or "none," when you changed nothing).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values on the Q8 scale (like the IS thresholds, {\f1 --ms-precision}, {\f1 --ms-bias}, {\f1 --ath-scale}, {\f1 --spread-mask}) are numbers where 256 means 1.0 \u8212- for example 243 is about 0.95.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part VIII. DAB+ Digital Radio\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 27. DAB+ output ({\f1 --dab}, {\f1 --dab-label})\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So far we've been making files \u8212- MP4/M4A for players, ADTS for streaming. DAB+ is something else: it's the format of terrestrial digital radio (the standard is ETSI TS 102 563), and this encoder can produce a ready DAB+ stream directly, without any external re-packing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why can't you just take an ordinary AAC file and send it over the air? Because DAB+ carries AAC in a way of its own. First, the transform is shorter \u8212- 960 samples instead of the usual 1024 \u8212- so the frame length fits the radio timing grid. Second, the audio is packed into a {\b super frame} that lasts exactly 120 milliseconds. Third, and most importantly, the air is a hostile place: the signal fades, cars drive through tunnels, interference comes and goes. So DAB+ wraps the sound in two layers of protection: a {\b firecode} (a Fire CRC) guarding the header, and {\b Reed-Solomon} error-correction coding \u8212- specifically RS(120,110) over the Galois field GF(256) \u8212- which lets the receiver rebuild data even when part of the signal arrives damaged. The encoder does all of this for you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What comes out is a raw {\f1 .dabp} stream: one super frame after another, nothing else. This is not a file you play in a media player \u8212- it's the raw material a DAB+ multiplexer eats. In practice the chain looks like this:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 out.dabp \u8594 ? odr-dabmux \u8594 ? ETI \u8594 ? transmitter (or a software receiver: welle.io, dablin)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 odr-dabmux} is the multiplexer that combines several stations into one ensemble and produces an ETI stream, which then goes either to a real transmitter or to a soft receiver for testing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Turning it on: {\f1 --dab}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac --dab -b 96 -o out.dabp input.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\f1 --dab} switch flips the encoder into DAB+ output mode. A few rules the standard forces on you, and the encoder enforces them:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The sample rate MUST be 32000 or 48000 Hz. Nothing else is legal in DAB+.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The bitrate must be a multiple of 8 kbps, from 8 up to 192 kbps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mono and stereo are both fine.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The interesting part is that you usually DON'T pick the profile yourself. DAB+ practice (and the reference tool {\f1 odr-audioenc}) is to derive the AAC profile from the bitrate and the channel count, so that low bitrates automatically use the more economical tools:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab stereo at 48 kbps or below \u8594 ? {\b HE-AAC v2} (with Parametric Stereo),\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab mono up to 64k, or stereo up to 80k \u8594 ? {\b HE-AAC} (with SBR),\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab above that \u8594 ? {\b AAC-LC} (the plain full-band profile).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So {\f1 -b 96} on a stereo file lands on AAC-LC, {\f1 -b 64} on HE-AAC, and {\f1 -b 32} on HE-AAC v2 \u8212- you don't have to think about it. If you insist, you can still force the profile with {\f1 -p} (2 = LC, 5 = HE-AAC, 29 = HE-AAC v2), the same as in every other mode of this encoder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac --dab -b 64 -o out.dabp input.wav    # \u8594 ? HE-AAC automatically\line
fdkaac --dab -b 32 -o out.dabp input.wav    # \u8594 ? HE-AAC v2 automatically\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The station label: {\f1 --dab-label}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A DAB+ receiver shows text on its display \u8212- the station name, and often the title of what's playing. In DAB+ that text travels in a channel called DLS (Dynamic Label Segment), tucked as X-PAD data inside the super frame. This encoder can carry a STATIC label \u8212- one fixed string for the whole file:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac --dab -b 96 --dab-label "Radio DHT" -o out.dabp input.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Up to about 48 characters fit (three segments packed into one PAD). "Static" is the key word: the text does not change over the course of the file. A truly dynamic label that updates track by track, and the MOT slideshow that puts album art on the screen (the job of {\f1 ODR-PadEnc} in the OpenDigitalRadio world), are on the roadmap but not here yet. And note: {\f1 --dab-label} without {\f1 --dab} does nothing \u8212- the label is simply ignored, because there is no super frame to put it in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 A word on trust\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Everything above was checked against independent tools, not just "it compiled." The streams decode cleanly in faad2 (the decoder behind dablin), the DLS label reads back correctly on the receiver side, and the AAC-LC output is bit-identical to what the reference {\f1 odr-audioenc} produces from the same input. Nine combinations were tested \u8212- 48 and 32 kHz, mono and stereo, across all three profiles \u8212- and each one decodes on its own. As everywhere in this encoder: if you don't pass {\f1 --dab}, nothing changes, and the ordinary file output stays bit-identical to stock FDK.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part IX. Glossary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For quick recall \u8212- all the terms from this manual, explained in one or two sentences, in the language of the audio engineer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AAC (Advanced Audio Coding). A family of lossy audio codecs from the MPEG family, the successor to MP3. Better quality per bit than MP3, especially at low bitrates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AAC-LC (Low Complexity). The basic AAC profile. Encodes the full band classically. The choice for high bitrates and archiving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Afterburner. An additional, slower algorithm optimizing quantization in FDK. On, it gives slightly better quality. On by default and recommended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 ATH (Absolute Threshold of Hearing). The threshold of hearing \u8212- the limit of silence dependent on frequency, below which the ear doesn't register sound. The encoder throws away what is below it. In this encoder regulated by {\f1 --ath-scale}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bitrate. The amount of data per second of sound, in kilobits (kbps) or bits (bps). Higher = more room for details = better quality (up to a certain limit).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bit reservoir. A buffer allowing bits to be borrowed between frames. Thanks to it even CBR locally "breathes." The basis of our quasi-VBR.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 CBR (Constant Bitrate). A fixed bitrate. Predictable size, convenient for streaming. In AAC slightly variable anyway thanks to the reservoir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Coupling (SBR). A stereo mode in the SBR layer in which both channels share a common envelope plus a level description \u8212- economical, but a narrower scene in the upper band.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Cutoff. The upper limit of the band encoded by the core. Above it either silence, or (in HE-AAC) SBR takes over the job.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HE-AAC (High Efficiency). AAC-LC + SBR. The core encodes the bottom of the band, SBR recreates the top. For medium and low bitrates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HE-AAC v2. HE-AAC + Parametric Stereo. For the lowest stereo bitrates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 ICC (Inter-channel Coherence). A PS parameter describing how similar/coherent the channels are to each other. Controlled by {\f1 --ps-icc}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 IID (Inter-channel Intensity Difference). A PS parameter describing the loudness difference between channels. The primary carrier of the stereo image in PS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Intensity Stereo (IS). A technique in which high bands are encoded as one energy envelope instead of two channels. Economical, but can narrow the scene. Controlled by the {\f1 --is-aggression} slider.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Inverse filtering. An SBR mechanism "whitening" the copied material of the upper band, controlled by a tonality estimator. Stronger = less metallicness. Regulated by {\f1 --sbr-invf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 IPD/OPD (phase differences in PS). NOT supported in the FDK encoder (always zero).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Quantization. Rounding spectrum coefficients to a finite precision. The fewer the bits, the larger the error (noise/holes). The heart of lossy compression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Masking. The phenomenon in which a loud sound makes you not hear quieter ones right next to it (in the spectrum or in time). The main source of bit saving in AAC.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MDCT. A transform carrying a frame of sound from time to the frequency domain. The whole psychoacoustic model works on its result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MS Stereo (Mid/Side). Encoding the sum (mid) and difference (side) instead of L and R. Almost free saving when the channels are similar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PNS (Perceptual Noise Substitution). Replaces noise bands with the description "there's noise of such energy here" instead of encoding them precisely. Controlled by {\f1 --pns}, {\f1 --force-pns}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Parametric Stereo (PS). Encodes one channel + parameters (IID, ICC), from which the decoder recreates stereo. For the lowest bitrates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Profile (AOT, Audio Object Type). The AAC variant selected by {\f1 -p} (LC, HE, v2, LD, ELD).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Frame. A portion of sound (1024 samples) that the encoder processes at once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 SBR (Spectral Band Replication). Reconstruction of the upper band from the lower plus a small description. The heart of HE-AAC.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 SFB (Scale Factor Band). A scale factor band. A group of neighboring spectrum coefficients for which the encoder makes a common decision about precision. Numbered from the bottom (0 = bass).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Spreading (of masking). The propagation of masking ability onto neighboring bands. Reduced by {\f1 --spread-mask} for more detail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tuning table. A set of default SBR/PS settings chosen by FDK by bitrate and frequency. Our flags override it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 TNS (Temporal Noise Shaping). Shapes the quantization noise in time, so it hides under transients. Important for percussion and sharp attacks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Transient. A sharp, short sound (a hit, an attack). Difficult for the codec, because the quantization noise can "outrun" the attack (pre-echo).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 VBR (Variable Bitrate). A variable bitrate dependent on the difficulty of the material. In FDK weak; our quasi-VBR (chapter 8) is a better alternative.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 End of glossary.\par}
